               <history>
                  <origin> <origDate when-custom="12XX" notBefore="1200" notAfter="1299"
                     calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii</origDate> </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_222">Garendon, Leicestershire.
                     Cistercian abbey of Saint Mary the Virgin</orgName><p><q>Liber sancte marie
                     [...]r[.]ld[...] si quis alienauerit anathema sit</q> (s. xiv) on verso of
                     flyleaf, facing the 1st page of text, near top. The 4th letter is e or o, the
                     first might be G. <q>Geroldonie</q> seems certain.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_422">Oxford,
                     Oxfordshire, Carmelite convent</orgName> <p>This attribution was rejected in
                     the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not
                     change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Library of Great
                     Britain Index card records that the source for this rejected attribution was
                     the <title>Summary Catalogue</title> II, i, 448-9. The relevant passage is:
                     <q>Pasted on fol. ii is a small piece of parchment regarding the gift of this
                     volume <q>per reuerendum magistrum fratrem Johannem Myluerton quondam
                     Prouincialem Anglie</q>, probably the Carmelite who was an Oxford doctor of
                     divinity in 1452: the volume may therefore have belonged to the library of the
                     Carmelites at Oxford earlier in the 15th century.</q></p></provenance>
               </history>
